Bellman’s Optimality Principle: 

For a policy to be truly optimal, it must have the property of every decision from the initial state must also be optimal. Regardless of how you break up the route of the optimal path, each individual substructure must be optimal in it of itself.  

 

Control Theory: 

Focuses on dynamic systems and how to choose inputs that will satisfy the desired objective. Usually involves observing the state and establishing controllers with the intention of creating stability and optimality.  

 

Open Loop: 

The control behind an action is pre-established and independent from the product of the action. Having the control set beforehand prevents the system from being adaptive and prone to errors in unexpected scenarios.  

 

Closed Loop (Feedback): 

The control behind an action is dependent on its state/output. Like a heating system turning off/on depending on the temperature of the area, it is heating. Tends to be the preferred option, especially in dynamic programming.  

 

Stability: 

We could consider a system stable if small disturbance only results in a small deviation and does not get out of control. In a better case, it would be asymptotically stable, which means that even with a small disturbance, the system will return back to its baseline. In this scenario, the system should always end up back at equilibrium.  

 

